I have been suffering horrible corruption on the serial line to my
MicroVAX II console (which is a PC, with Kermit). Having suspected the
cable, RFI problems etc. etc. I finally stumbled over the problem last
night. Removing and replacing the RS232 connector into the back of the
VAX, I could see a spark jumping from the plug shell to the chassis of
the VAX! I susbsequently connected the chassis of the MicroVAX to that
of the PC with a length of cable. Voila - no more corruption!

Question is, why does the case of the MicroVAX go live like this?
Testing with a multimeter suggests that is is earthed OK. The problem
does not lie with the PC, since other terminals exhibit the same
corruption!

I'm evidently not the first person to encounter this. Each component
of the MicroVAX system (which is intended for rack mount) has been
fitted with an earthing wire by DEC or the previous owners, which I
studiously ignored. To my mind this shouldn't be necessary. 

Comments anyone?

Cheers,

Bob.
