I have recently had to install an AlphaStation running Digital Unix 3.2C to
replace my trusty old DECstation 5000/50 running Ultrix 4.4.  Most of my mail
is sent via uucp, and I have run into what appears to be a bug in the way
the uux command sets up the control files for remote execution.  The D.xxx
control file that it creates contains something like the following:

S D.pan7c0c82b D.pan7c0c82b jw - D.pan7c0c82b 0666 jw
S rmail D.pan7c0c82b jw - D.0 0666 jw
S D.chsun21e882b X.chsunN21e7 jw - D.chsun21e882b 0666 jw

The first line is correct, but I believe that the second line is completely
bogus.  Because of the this line, when uucico runs it tries to transfer a
file named "rmail", which obviously doesn't exist.  This does not interfere
with mail delivery, but it causes me to get a complaint about "acces deined"
for each and every piece of mail that I send.  This gets old pretty quick...

I have checked this using the uux command manually, requesting other remote
commands, and the result is the same - the only difference is that the name
on the second line is whatever remote command has been requested.

Is this a bug in Digital Unix, as I suspect, or have I got something set
up wrong here?

jw



