The following is the lead story from the January 16th issue of Shannon 
Knows DEC. Enjoy.

DEC Gains Ground in the Benchmarking Wars

(c) 1996 by Terry C. Shannon

Long an also-ran in the benchmarketing wars, Digital Equipment 
Corporation has seized the initiative with a bevy of recently-announced 
benchmark results. At the low end, where Compaq is the Main Enemy, DEC 
extended its $/tpmC lead over its Austin, TX rival. And at the high end, 
where AlphaServer 8x00 systems vie with enterprise servers from HP, Sun, 
and SGI, DEC and its partners are effectively exploiting 64-bit Very 
Large Memory database technology.

In early December, Digital and Microsoft announced results of 3,194 tpmC 
at $196/tpmC on an AlphaServer 2100 5/300 with three CPUs running 
Microsoft SQL Server 6.5. One week later, the two companies announced 
what were billed as the world's best TPC-C benchmark price/performance 
results for a uniprocessor system. Using a AlphaServer 1000 4/266 
equipped with 1 GB of memory, WNT Server V3.51 and SQL Server V6.5, and 
Visigenics ODBC client software for SCO UNIX running on DEC Prioris PCs, 
the companies reported results of 1153.7 tpmC at $229/tpmC. Instead of 
comparing its results with those achieved by Compaq, Digital claimed that 
its results reflect better performance and price/performance than that 
offered by IBM's RS/6000 E20 and Sun's dual-processor SPARCserver 20.

Meanwhile at the high end, Oracle Corporation announced on December 6 
that it shattered the 10,000 tpmC open systems benchmark barrier--and 
leapfrogged the results it attained six weeks earlier--by achieving 
11,456 tpmC at $286/tpmC on an as-yet-unavailable 350 MHz TurboLaser. 
Oracle compared its latest benchmark with the Sybase SQL Server 11 result 
of 5,621 tpmC at $380/tpmC on a 12-CPU HP 9000 T500 server, and with the 
Informix Online 7.1 result of 6,313 tpmC at $479/tpmC on a 16-CPU SGI 
Challenge XL Server. Oracle's numbers were surpassed by two far more 
expensive systems. Using a 64-CPU Himalaya K10000 and Tandem NonStop SQL, 
Tandem achieved 12,021 tpmC at $1,150/tpmC. Tandem also managed to wring 
20,918 tpmC at $1,151/tpmC out of a 112-CPU Himalaya. DEC and Oracle aim 
to best Tandem with an EV6-based AlphaServer.

It didn't take long for Sybase to weigh in with a response to Oracle. On 
December 22, Sybase announced that its SQL Server11 database achieved the 
best TPC-C price/performance results ever recorded on the 300 MHz 
AlphaServer 8400. Using a 10-processor TurboLaser equipped with 6 GB of 
memory, Digital UNIX, and Sybase SQL Server 11, DEC and Sybase managed to 
achieve 11,014 tpmC at $222/tpmC. Not a bad response.

Sybase claimed that its numbers reflect a 17 percent speed improvement 
and 32 percent lower cost than Oracle's 300 MHz benchmark. While Sybase's 
price-and-performance on the 300 MHz TurboLaser bests Oracle's, a fair 
comparison can't be made until Sybase runs a benchmark on a 350 MHz 
TurboLaser.

SAP Superiority

DEC kicked off the new year by announcing record benchmark results on 
SAP's R/3 application suite. Running on a sextet of TurboLasers in a 
client/server configuration, the benchmark achieved throughput of 210,384 
SAP transactions per hour with an average dialog response time of 1.64 
sec. A total of 1,700 SD benchmark users was measured, performing 525,960 
dialog steps per hour. The benchmark environment was based on Digital 
UNIX V3.2C and Oracle V7.1.6.2.0 supporting SAP R/3 release 2.2D. The 
audited and certified results comply with SAP's recently-tightened "truth 
in benchmarking" rules. 

The client/server configuration included six 300 MHz AlphaServer 8400 
5/300 systems. The Oracle7 database server was equipped with 10 CPUs and 
4 GB of memory. Four application servers employed 12 CPUs with 4 GB of 
memory each, and one server employed 4 CPUs and 4 GB of memory. The 
benchmark fully utilized the CPU capacity of the application servers, and 
used 95 percent of the capacity of the database server. 

What Does It All Mean?

Although Digital finally has delivered TPC-C and SAP R/3 client/server 
benchmarks that take the wind out of HP's sails, DEC's Cupertino rival 
now claims that "performance isn't everything." There's an element of 
truth in this response. In order to achieve the highest benchmark 
results, DEC, HP, and every other vendor on the planet routinely use 
insanely large configurations that do not accurately reflect real-world 
computing environments. Accordingly, "your mileage may vary" and 
"benchmark your own applications" are two key caveats. Nevertheless, as 
DEC is sure to tell you, having good numbers in the benchmarketing wars 
definitely is a Good Thing.


