The following is the lead article for the January 1, 1996 issue of 
Shannon Knows DEC. Enjoy!

Fearless Prognostications for 1996

(c) 1996 by Terry C. Shannon
Editor, Shannon Knows DEC


It's that time again. Another year has shuffled off its mortal coils, and 
the supermarket tabloids are replete with New Year predictions from the 
crystal balls, tea leaves and Ouija boards of the world�s top psychics. 
Not to be bested by the tabloids, Shannon Knows DEC's crack team of 
clairvoyant canines and recovering rumourmongers has assembled a list of 
the Top Ten Digital Predictions for 1996. Mindful of the 
weather-guesser's "It's tough to predict, especially the future" caveat, 
SKD makes no guarantees as to the accuracy of these prognostications. But 
we can safely say that all of our 1996 predictions are much more likely 
to become future SKD headline items than are confabulations of the "Bob 
Palmer and Bill Gates Kidnapped By UFO from Mountain View" genre.

10) DEC Stock Hits the Century Mark

It's been a dog's age since Digital stock traded at 100 per share, but 
the firm's CY95 track record renders it likely that a share of DEC stock 
will set investors back a C-note during the coming year. Technology 
stocks increased about 50 percent in value during 1995, while DEC stock 
doubled during the same period. Digital's strong performance in the 
server marketplace, WNT's increasing contribution to the bottom line, and 
a revitalized corporate image all bode well for the firm and its 
investors. Barring unforeseen circumstances, the prospect of DEC stock at 
100 seems likely indeed.

9) Alpha Installed Base Burgeons

After a slow start, Alpha shipments are growing at a brisk rate. DEC 
shipped about $1.7B worth of Alpha systems in FY95; we anticipate that 
the FY96 Alpha take will exceed $2.5B. Based on shipment projections, we 
believe that the Alpha installed base will exceed 200K systems well 
before the end of the fiscal year. With over 100K workstations,10K 
low-end servers, 20K Sable servers, and 1K TurboLasers in the 
field, DEC is poised to pass the 200K milestone sooner rather than later.

8) Tombstone Territory

Although Alpha�s superior uniprocessor performance (and the 
less-than-overwhelming success of the circa-1987 VAXstation 35xx Firefox 
workstation) have led Digital to eschew SMP on the desktop, the firm in 
several months will debut an Alpha workstation that incorporates support 
for two EV56 processors. A quad-processor is also in the offing. With an 
estimated price in the neighborhood of $50K, the AlphaStation 700 
"Tombstone"  will vie with high-end Silicon Graphics workstations, as 
well as with SMP offerings from Sun Microsystems and Hewlett-Packard.

7) Lynx Boosts Sable Bandwidth

If there's an Achilles' Heel to the AlphaServer 2100 "Sable" lineup, it's 
I/O bandwidth. The imminent introduction of "Lynx," an upgraded Sable 
that offers more than twice the PCI I/O capacity of the incumbent, should 
address this deficiency and contribute to some impressive benchmark 
results. If you missed the inadvertent December '95 preview, look for 
Lynx to be on the prowl--and in the price books--later this month.

6) Rawhide: Not Just a TV Western

In Spring 1996, Digital will unveil "Rawhide," a successor to the 
AlphaServer 2100 Sable that features a higher-capacity backplane, 
improved I/O bandwidth, and support for faster EV5 and EV56 processors. 
The second-generation followon to the popular and profitable AlphaServer 
2100 should reassert DEC's leadership in the midrange SMP superserver 
arena, thus giving rival vendors a major case of heartburn. The 
substantial performance and throughput improvements offered by Rawhide 
will push Digital's midrange envelope and extend Windows NT support 
higher on the performance curve. The availability of WNT on an upscale 
midrange server will mitigate rival vendors' claims that DEC's failure to 
offer WNT on TurboLaser is proof that the firm is not serious about 
Microsoft's up-and-coming OS.

5) Dreaming in Double-Time

DEC's WNT-specific Alpha XL "Dream Machines" are slated for a performance 
upgrade within the next several months. The debut of an EV5-based Alpha 
XL desktop is likely to retire the incumbent 233 MHz entry-level system 
and establish the 266 MHz variant as the new low-end leader. Waiting in 
the wings are the first members of a family of economy-class WNT-based 
Dream Machine servers that will vie with low-end Pentium systems from 
Compaq, et al.

4) Curtailing Product Churn 

Despite an anticipated refresh of the entire Alpha workstation and server 
lineup in 1H96, customers can expect Digital to throttle back the pace of 
new product introductions. Initial evidence of moderating trends on the 
product rollout space is already in: DEC elected to forego the Fall 1995 
introduction of a 350 MHz TurboLaser server. Instead of offering an 
upgrade about six months after unveiling TurboLaser, DEC elected to defer 
the rollout of the AlphaServer 5/350 until early this year. Shortly after 
Hewlett-Packard unveils its PA8000- based enterprise server, DEC will 
respond with a TurboLaser whose ~400 MHz EV56 processors deliver a 30 to 
40 percent performance boost. A "go slower with faster processors" 
strategy is a likely characteristic of post-1H96 low-end and midrange 
refreshes as well.

3) DECUS Ascendant?

DEC's user group has been plagued by symposium attendance erosion for 
nearly half a decade. Although the prospect of once again encountering 
8,000-plus crowds at the twice-yearly pilgrimage is unlikely at best, 
look for Digital to renew its commitment to DECUS under the stewardship 
of VP Nancy Strecker. A DECUS-Digital "affinity strategy" that renders 
the group more attractive to partners and competitors is a likely element 
of the revitalization campaign.

2) Back On the Radar Screen 

During the past several years, rival vendors have regarded Digital as a 
pitiful giant whose primary asset was an installed base replete with 
"migration opportunities." DEC's return to financial health, the adoption 
of a rationalized OS strategy, and a generous dose of professional 
marketing have ended the firm's role as a low-observable in the IT 
industry. Competitors who claim not to have seen Digital in their 
accounts for quite some time are all but certain to renew their 
acquaintance during the year ahead.

1) A Trio of Trends

Our Number One prediction is that WNT, 64-bitness, and the Linux OS will 
be hot topics in 1996. Among the major IT vendors, Digital appears to be 
best positioned to capitalize on all three of these hot buttons. For 
Digital, 1996 is shaping up as a very interesting year! 





