In article <310B4C5A.25FD@alpes-net.fr>, Gilles  <gmollie@alpes-net.fr> wrote:
>I've read a pentium pro at 200 Mhz ran faster on interger 
>instruction than a 21164 at 300 Mhz, is it right ?

Well, Intel's initial SPEC92 number of 366 SPECint92 definitely bested 
the 300 MHz EV5. After discovering an error in their benchmarking, Intel 
released a revised figure of 318.4 SPECint92 for the 200 MHz Pentium Pro. 
Digital subsequently ran benchmarks with a better-optimized compiler and 
got 341.4 SPECint92 from the 300 MHz chip. Accordingly, Alpha remains the 
leader.

On the other hand, your applications won't run any faster as a result of 
DEC's new benchmarks. And if you had a Pentium Pro system, Intel's ~15 
percent lower revised benchmark wouldn't slow your apps down by as much 
as a nanosecond.

The Pentium Pro is good stuff. I'll bet that the folks at PowerPC don't 
feel that way, though!

As for Alpha, here's the latest info on the 333 and 350 MHz EV5 variants.

MHz	SPECint92	SPECfp92

333	412.4		545.2		(measured on AlphaStation 600 5/333)
350	432.8		602.2		(measured on AlphaServer 8x00 5/350)



