In article <4ecc5u$nti@news.alaska.edu>, fncll@aurora.alaska.edu (Chris L.) 
writes:
|>
|>I bought a used DECpc LPx 433dx. I read in the users manual that it was
|>upgradable to 486 and Pentium Overdrive chips. However, while it went to a
|>486-DX2-66 just fine, when I got my hands on a Pentium chip, it is way too
|>large for the socket. What is the deal here? Is it upgradeable to a Pentium
|>or is a Pentium Overdrive a different beast altogether? 
|>
|>If anyone has any information that would help me, I would greatly
|>appreciate it!

Yes, a Pentium Overdrive is very definitely a different beast. It has an
integral 5V-3.3V converter and the socket pin layout is compatible with the
486.

You may find, though, that the LPx will not run with a Pentium Overdrive.
If you try it, be sure that you can return the chip if necessary.  Intel
encouraged manufacturers to claim Overdrive compatibility long before the
chip existed, and it turned out not to be as compatible as people thought.
-- 

