ss10@irz.inf.tu-dresden.de (Sebastian Schoenberg) wrote:
>
>
>Hi,
>
>I read the 21064 manual, but I cannot believ in this that two completely independent integer
>instructions cannot be executed in parallel, like
>
>	ornot	zero, zero, a1	// Load a1 with 0xffffffffffffffff
>	bis	zero, zero, a2	// Load a2 with 0
>
>Is this only for older 21064 AXP or is it in general ?
>

This is a pipelined CPU, so both instructions can be in various stages of 
execution at the same time (provided there are no conflicts).  The 21064
is able to ISSUE (or start the execution) of 1 integer and 1 floating 
point instruction in the same cycle.  Previously issued instructions 
may still be in the pipeline so more than 1 instruction will be 
executing.  Other implementations of the Alpha architecture are able to 
issue more than 1 integer and more than 1 floating point instruction on 
the same cycle (I believe the 21164 is able to issue 2 of each, but I 
don't have the specs in front of me so I may be wrong).  I believe a load 
instruction can also be issued at the same time as either an integer or
floating point instruction, but again the issue rules are not in front of 
me and I may be wrong (it's been a few months since I last look at the 
rules).

