   The MicroVAX I is limited to 4 MB of (Q-bus-based MSV11) memory,
   though you can probably get as much as (circa) 150 MB of RD54 disk
   hanging off an appropriate RQDX-series controller.  The video
   controller used in the VAXstation I is the VCB01, and it requires
   what is now a non-standard monitor video format.

   You will much happier with an old MicroVAX 3100 or VAXstation 3100,
   or a VAXstation 4000 VLC, or (if you absolutely need a Q-bus) a
   MicroVAX II or VAXstation II, or a VAXstation 3200 or VAXstation
   3500 series.  (I'd aim for one of the VAXstation 3100 or low-end
   VAXstation 4000 series such as the VLC.)  All of these systems are
   more common than the MicroVAX I, all are more expandable, and all
   are at least triple the CPU performance...  And unlike the MicroVAX
   I, all these systems are supported by current versions of OpenVMS...

