references: <4dbo8u$e5t@nntp5.u.washington.edu> <4dgqhl$73t@news.duke.edu> <4dps33$205@src-news.pa.dec.com> <1996Jan21.101138.71817@cc.usu.edu>
organization: DEC Systems Research Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <1996Jan21.101138.71817@cc.usu.edu>, ivie@cc.usu.edu (Roger Ivie) writes:
> Strictly from adding up cycles in the data book. I don't have my notes in
> front of me at the moment, though. It looked to me from the data book that
> in order to exceed 40MB/s on a 21072-based system, the data had to be both
> mapped in the 21072's scatter/gather TLB and also be in the processor cache.
> This is for 8-longword burst reads; the timing for writes looked to be to
> complicated to figure out from the data book (the chipset has write buffers,
> so performance depends on other things going on in the system). Since the
> data books give no timing diagrams for 64-bit memory systems, my estimates
> were for the 128-bit memory path.

I haven't studied the TLB load timings.  My numbers were measurements on
a 400 4/233 (64 bits wide) and a 1000 4/200 (128 bits).


It's possible that the environment I was running in didn't use the TLB.

You need it for either of two reasons.

One is so hardware that only knows about 32 bit addressing can read/write
memory beyond 32 bits. (Round down because the IO devices need some
addresses too.)  That's not a problem for most workstations since they
can't hold that much memory.

The other is so that the device can do a large stream of transfers to 
contigious (virtual) addresses that don't happen to be contigious in
physical memory.  The board/firmware I was working with breaks things up
into page size chunks and goes through a V=>P mapping when setting up the
DMA address for each chunk so it doesn't need the TLB to do it again.


The other possibility is TLB loads don't cost much.  I was doing mostly
page size (8K) transfers.  If a TLB load covers the whole page and costs as
much as a 32 byte data access to memory, then it adds roughly 1/2 % overhead.

I only had 2 or 3 devices transfering at the same time so TLB churning
shouldn't be a problem.



Writing is usually faster than reading because the pipeline is in the
right direction.



