We are looking to buy serveral high-end alpha systems (or 
a rack-mounted system), possibly from one of the clone
makers. I would like advice and suggestions on the vendor,
the system, and the OS.

We need as much floating point capability as $80k can buy.
We don't need any graphics (other than a console). We don't
need any large disk space either (1GB/node is probably enough).
We need a fast ethernet. Some decent amount of RAM is prefered
(maybe 128MB/node or more). From the quotes we got from Neko Tech
and Carrera, it seems that we should be able to buy about 6
21164 300MHz level machines.

We don't really care what OS is run, but would prefer a version
of Unix. A high quality, reliable, and efficient fortran compiler
is a must. (Does that rule out Linux?). Our fortran code uses
extensively double precision complex arithmetic. Unlimited
multiple remote sessions (such as xterm) is also required.

My questions are:
1. How reliable (products and service) are these
companies. Are there vendors that you would recommend?
2. How do the clones compare to the DEC machines both in terms
of quality and speed?
3. How do Digital Unix, Linux, and NT compare in view of our needs?
My uneducated guess would be that Linux lacks a good fortran compiler
(I know that our code does not compile under current g77). I am
also concerned about the speed of NT.
4. One vendor told us that DEC disables a register on the ALPHA
chips they sell to the clone makers for the UNIX machines. And then
they sell them for $3k more than the NT machines. Is this true?

Any advice and comments are welcome. You will help save some
taxpayers' money. Thanks.

Xiaoguang Zhang
Oak Ridge National Laboratory
xgz@ornl.gov



