In article <4dps33$205@src-news.pa.dec.com>, murray@pa.dec.com (Hal Murray) writes:
> In article <1996Jan17.094806.71471@cc.usu.edu>, ivie@cc.usu.edu (Roger Ivie) writes:
> 
>>                                                  Neptune-based systems
>> top out around 50MB/s and it looks to me like you need all the planets
>> to align correctly to get more than 40MB/s out of a 21072-based system.
> 
> 
> What sort of IO gear were you using?  What size bursts were you reading
> or writing?

Strictly from adding up cycles in the data book. I don't have my notes in
front of me at the moment, though. It looked to me from the data book that
in order to exceed 40MB/s on a 21072-based system, the data had to be both
mapped in the 21072's scatter/gather TLB and also be in the processor cache.
This is for 8-longword burst reads; the timing for writes looked to be to
complicated to figure out from the data book (the chipset has write buffers,
so performance depends on other things going on in the system). Since the
data books give no timing diagrams for 64-bit memory systems, my estimates
were for the 128-bit memory path.
> 
> I routinely get over 60 megabytes/sec on 64 bit systems and over 70
> on 128 bit systems.  That's using 64 byte DMA bursts when testing ATM
> adapters.

I don't have hardware lying around to do throughput testing on PCI (my
throughput testing hardware on TURBOchannel consisted of four 8-bit
bidirectional registered transceivers and a 29CPL154 microsequencer; to
build similar hardware for PCI, I'd have to have a silicon foundry to do
the bus drivers), so for the moment it's just been counting cycles in the
data book.
