Thanks to all who replied. I finally got the drive to work, but I'm
not sure what I did that made a difference.

First, I *didn't* make any changes to the kernel. I reconfigured the
kernel and rebooted the system *before* trying to add the tape drive.

Second, just to clarify: the problem was never that the system didn't
recognize the drive as a SCSI device. Rather, it seemed that none of
the device files created by MAKEDEV corresponded to the Sony drive, as
the output of "mt -t <device> stat" was the same regardless of which
device I specified.

Third, after rebooting the system yet again, I found that I could
create valid devices for the drive by running MAKEDEV by hand... at
least, the drive would load a tape *once* after rebooting if MAKDEV
was run by hand. After the tape was unloaded, it would not load
another tape until the next reboot. (I tried "mt online" but it
returned an error.)

Finally, after running scu(8), sticking in a tape, and doing a mt load
explicitly from the scu prompt, I am now able to eject and reload
tapes many times without rebooting. The /dev/rmt0? and /dev/mt0?
devices auto-created at boot time also work now, though I haven't been
trying them regularly, so I don't know when they started working.

Does this make sense to anyone ??

(To be sure, I can't rule out the possibility that scu has nothing to
do with anything, and the system only needed N reboots after
reconfiguring the kernel, where N > 2. If so, I'd be grateful to
anyone who could tell me what N actually is, and why.)

