you certainly forgot to actually make the filesystem on the disk! As you
are still bale to mount a filesystem, I suppose it is an 'a' partition
which was made earlier on this disk. To be sure to have a good partition 
table on the disk proceed as follows:

newfs /dev/rrz0a rz57	#makes a new partition and a new superblock on
			#the beginning of the disk (You now have the
			#superblock to write the partitiontable in

chpt -d /dev/rrz0c
chpt -a /dev/rrz0c	#write a default partition table to disk

chpt -p# #### ##### /dev/rrz0c	#optionally adapt the partition table, you
				#probably won't do this

newfs /dev/rrz0c rz57	#make a filesystem on the whole disk

mount...
df...
Ok... ;-)

This proceeding is probably overkill, but you always need to be sure to
have the partition table written to disk. This for you need to have
a valid superblock on the partition beginning at blocknr. 0

Andreas

