In article <4dvvaa$6l7@nx2.hrz.uni-dortmund.de>, uch435@zx2.HRZ.Uni-Dortmund.DE (Jens Cardinal) writes:

:But what are my options if I want to use such a drive with my
:VAXstation 2000 ? I think I need a TK50-DB, right ? Do I need anything
:else to get going ? And finally is anybody out there willing to part
:with such a beast ? I would prefer to buy from someone in Germany or
:Europe but am willing to make arrangements for a deal world wide :-)

  The VAXstation 2000 needs the BN40A external expansion adapter.  This
  is externally visible as a roughly two centimeter extension mounted on
  the bottom of the VAXstation 2000 enclosure.  (If somebody ripped the
  electronics out of the box and left the extension mounted, all bets
  are off.)  I'll assume you have this...

  You will want to use the TK50Z-Fx -- I don't believe the TK50-DB was
  ever supported for (or ever worked with/on) the VAXstation 2000.  The 
  -F3 variant is the 240V variant.  The TK50Z-Fx drive was sometimes
  ROM-upgraded and hardware-tweaked to transform it for use as the SCSI
  TK50Z-Gx, and you _may_ be able to get the -G3 variant to work on the
  non-SCSI VAXstation 2000.  (The VAXstation 2000 requires the TK50Z
  drive be selected for ID 1 -- the -Gx is usually at ID 5.  The -Gx
  variant will _probably_ work in place of the -Fx, though this is not
  supported.  This -Fx/-Gx information should also not be construed as
  indicating the VAXstation 2000 expansion bus is SCSI -- it isn't.)

