Every RD53/RD54 I have ever seen has made odd noises at some stage in its
life. Of the twenty or so I have dealt with only one has ever died on me.

|>There are three similar sized expansion cabinets, containing one heavy
|>Fujitsu M2333K (8038-4765-8003A) drive each, together with power
|>supply.
|>
|>Problem is, both machines originally had a TK50 drive. Neither of
|>these seems to work. Basically the drive refuses to load the tape in
|>the first place. No attempt is made to spool the tape into the drive,
|>and the fault indication (rapidly flashing red light) is asserted. I
|>wonder if I have exaccerbated the problem by removing a tape when I
|>shouldn't have done so ... the manual suggests that the drive may be
|>screwed up by inopportune replacement of tapes. 

TK50 drives have a reputation for being a little fragile. Usually you screw them
up by pulling the cartridge out and leaving streams of tape everywhere. Two duff
ones seems a little unfortunate. What is the history here? Are they likely to be 
broken? One way to get the problem is to put the wrong type of cratridge in.
Make sure that the cartridge is a TK50K (CompactTape) and not a 
TK52K (CompactTape II). The latter is designed for a TK70 drive. If you use one
in a TK50 and try to load it (press red button) you get the symptoms you
describe (rapid flashing - i.e. error). Note that it is possible to use a TK50K
tape in a TK70 drive and initialise it and use it (possible, but not 
recommended). If someone has done this to your tape, it will again give the
same symptoms even though. See if you can find known good TK50 (one you have 
seen working in a TK50 drive) and try that in your drives. Send me mail if
you get stuck (I might be able to point you towards some people ...)

>Does anyone know anything much about TK50s or even know where I could
>obtain another one, either by purchase or loan, in the UK? I'm a
>little stuck trying to install any software without one!

TK50 tape drives are a little expensive as yet (i.e. they are not free :-) but
I don't think you need give up on yours quite yet.

>Naturally, I want to make one `best I can' machine out of these two by
>swapping baords from one to the other. Any hints on what I can move
>where, and what the boards actually are would be most appreciated!

Assuming all the boards work, I don't think you can improve things by moving
boards around. Now if you can get a KA650 and some MS650 memory, then things
might fly a little better (three times better in fact).

