In article <4dgqhl$73t@news.duke.edu>, gallatin@renoir.isds.duke.edu (Andrew Gallatin) writes:

> The 800 has the advantage of a 2MB cache, as opposed to the 0.5MB
> cache on the 233 & and it has two builtin SCSI busses, as opposed to
> one on the 233. It has the disadvantage of having a turbochannel bus,
> which is slower than PCI & has fewer (and more expensive) expansion
> options available for it.

The TURBOchannel may be slower on paper (100 megabytes vs 132) but
it supports long bursts while most PCI implementations don't.  The
actual total throughput may be backwards from what you expect if you
just look at the burst rate.

The memory system in the 3000-800 has roughly twice the bandwidth of
the one in the 400 4/233.

The 3000-800 has 6 TC slots vs 3 PCI slots in the 400 4/233.  (The
400 4/233 also has 3 ISA slots and one of the PCI slots is a combo.)

TC is over the hill, but the 3000-800 is a fine machine with a top of the
line TC implementation.  Don't run away just because the IO bus is old
fashioned.



The 3000-800 is quite a bit bigger and probably takes more power
and makes more heat/noise.


The 3000-800 uses the 21064 CPU chip while the 400 4/233 uses the 21064A.
That "A" means the on chip cache is 16K each for I/D vs 8K.

(The web page for the 3000-800 below doesn't specify the on chip cache
size so please check this if it is important.)



Maybe helpful web pages:

3000-800:
  http://www.digital.com/info/Customer-Update/931012006.txt.html

400 4/233
  http://www.digital.com/info/Customer-Update/941103003.txt.html  

There is a link to a search engine at the bottom of those pages.

Alta Vista may be easier to use and/or faster.  It will find things
that are not on Digital's web server.



