X-Deja-AN: 135924252
distribution: world
references: <ERIC.96Jan10155942@goonsquad.spies.com> <4d96du$jb0@info-server.bbn.com>
organization: Kernel Mode Systems, San Diego, CA
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <4d96du$jb0@info-server.bbn.com>, bkort@labs-n.bbn.com () writes:
> Eric Smith (eric@goonsquad.spies.com) wrote:
> 
> : I just bought a VAXstation 3520.  I'm not sure if I got a good deal or
> : not, but I didn't pay too much.
> 
> : Can anyone tell me the general specs?  Which generation of VAX CPU
> : does it use?  

It has two CPUs, each the same CPU chip as the VAXstation 3500/3600,
2.8 VUPs each. 

> : Are there newer plug-compatible VAX CPU cards I could
> : put in to upgrade it?

no.

> 
> : I didn't get a monitor with it; which DEC monitors are compatible?

Depends on which video card set it has, but it's probably
GPX-compatible, meaning you're looking at the VR290, 299, etc.
1024x864, 60 Hz vertical, 54 kHz horizontal.  A modern multisync PC
monitor should work fine, as long as it has BNC inputs and supports
sync on green. 

> 
> That's a dual CPU version of the 3500, which uses the KA650 CPU,
> same as the VAXserver 3600 and MicroVAX III.

Correction.  It uses the same CPU *chip* as the KA650.  The KA650 is a
CPU *card* that was indeed used in the VAXserver/VAXstation/MicroVAX
3500 and 3600.  (Further correction: There was never a machine that 
was officially called the MicroVAX III.)

The CPU card in the 3520 actually carries two CPU chips... pretty 
different from the KA650.  (And the Vaxstation 3540 had two such 
cards, total of four CPU chips.)

> You can bring it up with a VT100 console plugged into the MMJ
> connector on the CPU if you don't have monitor.
> 
> It's a decent machine.

A pair of 2.8 VUPs CPUs, only a few free IO slots, which is just as well
because not many Q-bus drivers were ever made SMP-safe, etc...  Last decade,
it was a decent machine... however it was always a strange beast, not 
very well supported.  

if you just want a VAX/VMS machine in the lab to use for testing in an SMP
environment, it'll do.  I wouldn't count on hosting any heavy 
development or applications on it, though.

