references: <4dbo8u$e5t@nntp5.u.washington.edu> <4dgqhl$73t@news.duke.edu> <1996Jan17.094806.71471@cc.usu.edu>
organization: DEC Systems Research Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <1996Jan17.094806.71471@cc.usu.edu>, ivie@cc.usu.edu (Roger Ivie) writes:

>                                                  Neptune-based systems
> top out around 50MB/s and it looks to me like you need all the planets
> to align correctly to get more than 40MB/s out of a 21072-based system.


What sort of IO gear were you using?  What size bursts were you reading
or writing?


The 21071 version of that chip set has a 64 bit data path to memory
and the 21072 has 128 bits.

I routinely get over 60 megabytes/sec on 64 bit systems and over 70
on 128 bit systems.  That's using 64 byte DMA bursts when testing ATM
adapters.


Those are benchmark numbers from hardware test programs.  Don't expect
to get that sort of throughput if you have an OS to "help".  Besides,
there probably aren't many memory cycles left over for running
OS or application code.



