organization: Worcester Polytechnic Institute
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

>>>>> "Ron" == Ron Natalie <ron@topaz.sensor.com> writes:
In article <4dgoih$ue2@topaz.sensor.com> ron@topaz.sensor.com (Ron Natalie) writes:


Ron> Seung-Jo Baek. (sjbaek@vivace.postech.ac.kr) wrote:
Ron> : Hi. DEC users..
Ron> : I'm curious how to setup a new SCSI device on AlphaStation200-4/233
Ron> : running DigitalUnix V3.3C...
Ron> : I've tried to connect 233MB removable Bernoulli disk(IOMEGA) to
Ron> : this system...
Ron> : I've set SCSI #, external termination etc..
Ron> : But, when I mount it to /mnt(as followings), "no such device...."
Ron> : messages
Ron> : %mount /dev/rrz5a /mnt
Ron> :     (SCSI number of IOMEGA disk was 5)
Ron> : Any ideas?
Ron> : Thans in advance...
Ron> : Have a nice day~

Ron> First off, you don't want to use the raw device for mount (ever), it
Ron> doesn't use that.  You wan to use the cooked device (/dev/rz5a).

Ron> Second, you need to make sure that the device has been in the kernel
Ron> (look in /sys/conf for your kernel).

Ron> Third, make sure that you've made the appropriate device with MAKEDEV
Ron> (DEC UNIX follows the BSD loosy-goosiness between dev names and scsi'id's
Ron> so you need to check the minor device numbers).  Good info can be had
Ron> by saying "man rz"

Fourth, label the disk with 'disklabel -rw rz5 rz5'.

Fifth, put a UFS filesystem on the disk with the newfs command.  You
could use Advfs on the disk, but since it creates links in /etc/fdmns,
you would have to make sure you set it up correctly and you might run
into problems if you're not careful.

Sixth, mount the disk with 'mount /dev/rz5c /mnt' and away you go.  

Digital Unix doesn't have explicit support for removeable media, so
you'll have to be root to mount and unmount it as needed.  

John

