> I'm not sure of the 3100/38, but you may need special mounting
> hardware to attach a new drive, but there is usually a spare
> scsi connecter and power connector laying around inside.
> 
> It's usually quite easy to add a new drive once you've gotten
> the correct mounting hardware.

  There's two different flavors of mounting hardware/baseplates. One where
you have to remove the baseplate and install special rubber spacers with
bolts in 'em, and one where you can just slide the drive (with rubber feet
attached) and it'll snap in on its own.

  If you have the latter style, you can make your own rubber feet for a 
generic drive with parts you get at Radio Shack - you'll need a bag of
assorted rubber grommets and some 6/32 x 1.4" machine screws. I believe
it's the next-to-the-smallest grommet size you need. Put a 6/32 screw 
through the hole in the grommet and screw this whole assembly to the
bottom of the disk drive you want to mount. Repeat for the other 3 mount-
ing holes. Now slide the drive onto the baseplate until the spring catches
and you're all set.

