In article <4dgqhl$73t@news.duke.edu>, gallatin@renoir.isds.duke.edu (Andrew Gallatin) writes:
> 
> It has the disadvantage of having a turbochannel bus,
> which is slower than PCI & has fewer (and more expensive) expansion
> options available for it.

Although on paper TURBOchannel is slower (100MB/s peak versus 132MB/s peak),
you'd be hard pressed to find a PCI box which can out-DMA that old
DECstation 5000/200 sitting in the corner. The measurements I made on
a DECstation 5000/200 many years ago put it at just over 50MB/s (the
worst case time I saw for reading a single longword from memory was
2.7 microseconds. Adding 2.52 microseconds for the remaining portion
of a 256-byte transfer gives just under 20ns/byte (there is no provision
for stalling a DMA transfer once it's started, and the motherboard doesn't
know the length of a DMA transfer until it's over, so you will actually get
the rest of a 256-byte transfer in 2.52 microseconds)). Neptune-based systems
top out around 50MB/s and it looks to me like you need all the planets
to align correctly to get more than 40MB/s out of a 21072-based system.

Yes, the newest PCI systems can outperform TURBOchannel systems. But the
TURBOchannel systems they are outperforming are five years old...
