Dear Keith,

     Brian is right about the spin error.  However the most likely source
of the problem is a siezed bearing on the drive or motor.  This is a very
common problem of most drives that are never turned off for a couple or
more years, then allowed to spin down and cool for several hours.  We have
been successful with the older 5 1/4 inch drives by manually spining the
shaft (when exposed) or shaking/rapping the drive while repeatedly applying
power (Not a recommended practice, but you get desparate when you have
data that is not backed up on one of these drives.) 

     We got rid of our 750 a few years ago, and when we had the spin failures,
our DEC service contract replaced the drives, but it was from the service
technician that we learned of the cause of the failure.  I can't tell you
if you can start it spinning while power is applied, but try spinning it
with it apart.  Then try again.

     Basically you have to decide (even on newer drives) if it is going to be
running 24 hours a day, then never turn it off ever - except for power fails,
or turn it off regularly (ie. every night).

     Good luck.

Gary Muswick.



