Or, system admin utility like "vmubc" is available from DEC ftp server.
Followings are README file from vmubc packages.

-------- README for Vmubc-----------------
This directory contains source and executables for vmubc.  vmubc
displays
Universal Buffer Cache, CPU and VM statistics, updating every second. 

You must be root to install this!

vmubc displays CPU utilization for SMP systems -- this means that
an executable built for DEC OSF/1 V2.x will NOT work correctly on
DEC OSF/1 V3.0, as the V2.x system header files don't define the
appropriate data structures.  Oddly enough, an executable built on
DEC OSF/1 V3.0 will work correctly on DEC OSF/1 V2.x.

If you're root, VmUBC will run just fine in *this* directory, or it
can be installed so that anyone on the system can run it.
Type "make install" to install it in on your system -- you must be
root to install it.  "make install" does the following:

	cp vmubc.uid /usr/lib/X11/uid/vmubc.uid
	chmod 444 /usr/lib/X11/uid/vmubc.uid
	cp vmubc /usr/bin/X11/vmubc
	chmod 2555 /usr/bin/X11/vmubc
	chown root:mem /usr/bin/X11/vmubc


vmubc can also generate output to a tty instead of an X11 display; the
-tty option enables this.


