In article <DLC1ux.7u3@csc.liv.ac.uk>, Eddy Austin <edward@liv.ac.uk> writes:

:I now have got to the stage where I have connected a VT terminal to the
:VAXstation 3100/38 console port with an mmj<->rs232 connector borrowed from
:a VAXserver 3400.
:
:However I get no response from the vt220, am I right to believe that I need
:to connect 2 lines together on the connector so that the 3100/38 recognises
:I want to boot from the VT as opposed to say a VR260 graphics monitor and
:kbd???

  One does not "boot from the VT", one "uses the VT as a console".
  In this case, there is a switch -- labeled S3 -- that may need to
  be flipped.  If you are getting no response from the VT, you likely
  have some sort of a wiring problem.  If you get garbled responses,
  you have a baud-rate mis-match.

:I'm getting no response from the 3100/38, is the connector wiring at fault?

  Very likely, though it could be dead hardware.

:Also I have VMS licences, how can I download VMS from the 3400 onto the
:3100/38 via thinwire? vmskitbld? how do I get this and how do I get it to
:work???

  Assuming you have the appropriate licenses, and that you also have
  access to CLUSTER_CONFIG and a clustering license, configure the node
  as a satellite of the MicroVAX 3400 and boot it via the network. 
  Otherwise, you'll have to locate some sort of interchange media common
  to the two systems, and create a BACKUP that can be restored via
  standalone BACKUP.  (Without something like DECnet running on the
  VAXstation 3100 model 38 end of the connection, half the network
  necessary for the file transfers is missing...)

