In article <4dc20v$jjv@Mercury.mcs.com>, fluke@MCS.COM (Greg Linder) writes:
:	I recently came into possession of a VAXstation II/GPX system.
:Unfortunately, the machine does not boot. When I power it up, I recieve two 
:beeps, and then it just sits there.
:	The first few times I powered it up, the lighted button on the tape
:drive flashed, and, after I pushed it in, just kept on flashing.
:	Then, I turned to the back of the system, powered it down, and started
:trying different boot configurations of the rotary  knob on the back(with the
:arrow, head, and T in a circle on it) and the little switch with a circle with
:the dot in it, and a circle with the dot out of it. I haven't the faintest idea
:what any of that means, but at the moment the switch is set to the circle with
:the dot in it, and the knob is set to the downward arrow.
:	All I want to know is how to get this thing to boot. It has a hard
:drive in it, but it does not seem to spin up correctly(most drives click when
:they spin up- this one does not)

  This thing likely has one or more very low-level configuration problems
  (internal cabling, Q-bus, etc), or it has a processor hardware error. 
  Connect a serial terminal to the console port, flip the paddle switch
  to dot-in-circle, press <BREAK> a couple of times, if nothing happens,
  turn the system off and back on, and look for a count-down self-test
  sequence.  If you do not see a countdown, check the console bulkhead
  for the countdown, as well.

