In article <4dbo8u$e5t@nntp5.u.washington.edu>, S. Debruynkops <debk> wrote:
>I have the option to buy a used 3000/800 for something less than the price of a
>new 400 4/233 with comparable features.  The machines benchmark about the same
>for my intensive floating point computations.  Typical code consumes 200+ Mb
>memory and runs for several days with perhaps one interactive user also on the
>system.  Operating system is UNIX.  I'd appreciate comments on the relative
>merits of the different bus architectures or other points.   --Steve
>


The 800 has the advantage of a 2MB cache, as opposed to the 0.5MB
cache on the 233 & and it has two builtin SCSI busses, as opposed to
one on the 233. It has the disadvantage of having a turbochannel bus,
which is slower than PCI & has fewer (and more expensive) expansion
options available for it.

The new 233 will almost certainly have a *much* better warranty. If
its like the 200 series, then its got a socketed CPU, allowing you the
possibility to upgrade the CPU if they ever come out with CPUs faster
than 233Mhz that fits that socket.

In case you feel the need to support multiple OSes, they'll both run
Digital UNIX, OpenVMS & NetBSD/alpha.

Drew

