In article <wwv20pb57cv.fsf@axp01.mrc-bbc.ox.ac.uk>,
Ray.Bellis@psy.ox.ac.uk (Ray Bellis) writes:
|>A client of mine wishes to connect a pool of modems (proably V.34 USR
|>Sportsters) to an Alpha 3000/600.  Are the limited modem-control
|>lines
|>on the 16-port DECserver 700 really sufficient for these sorts of
|>modem?
|>
|>I've not used LAT myself before but I gather that it'll allow each
|>port on the DECserver to look exactly as if it was a physical
|>hardware
|>port (with an individual entry in /dev) so that almost any software
|>which uses a /dev/tty can use a remote port instead.  Is that
|>correct?
|>
|>Thanks,
|>
|>Ray.

Limited modem control is not really a good term here.  We got into using it
when the European PTT's were pretty fussy about number of modem signals
they required in order to attach to their networks.  "Full Modem
Control" is still required in some applications, but for yours, the
16-Port DECserver 700 will do just fine.  We use "partial modem control"
with the high end modems (9600 baud and above) big time with no
problems.

If you are buying the cables and adapters from Digital I recommend the
BN25G cable and the H8585-AC adapter.

Loren



