In article <DKxuoF.DtE@ampex.com>, chungto@ampex.com (Tony Chung) writes:
|>The file .mrg..files.dat was mentioned in /sys/conf/apha/files:
|>
|># OSF/1 Release 1.0 (Alpha files)
|>#
|># !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
|># !!! NOTE TO DEVELOPERS !!!
|># !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
|>#
|># IF YOU MODIFY THIS FILE, YOU MUST ADD THE CORRESPONDING CHANGES TO
|># ".mrg..files.dat", ALSO CONTAINED IN THIS DIRECTORY. FAILURE TO
|># UPDATE ".mrg..files.dat" WILL RESULT IN PROBLEMS DURING UPDATE
|># INSTALLATION !
|>#
|>
|>I could not find any info in both manuals: System Adminstraction and
|>Writing Device Drivers:Tutorial.
|>Currently, I just ignore this file.

This file was used (it's obsolete in V4.0) to define that changes made in the
"files" file from release N-1 to release N and was used during an update
installation procedure to merge these changes into the existing files file.

As a developer of kernel products, there are mechanisms other than directly
modifying the files file that you should use.  So, continue to ignore this file.

In case you are still interested, this file was read by the .mrg..files script 
to perform the merge.

|>
|>Another question: maxusers was set to 32 but additional users
|>are refused to rlogin when there are only few.
|>How do I find out the actual setting and change it if
|>necessary?
|>

maxusers does not override the amount of users licensed through your OSF-BASE
and OSF-USR PAKs.  An "lmf list" should show something like this:

Product                   Status                     Users: Total      Active

OSF-USR                   active                            unlimited

