Dear Julian,

  What's your price range?  What is your prime concern, ease of use
or raw speed?  Is your application floating point or mostly integer
operations?  Are you sure you want a RISC box and not a P6?  How much
memory will your runs use?  What langmuage are you programming in?
(You may have to pay $1k-2k extra for a compiler!)

  Anyway, here are some SPEC benchmarks for some Alphas (and pentiums):

System            CPU        ClkMHz  Cache      SPECint SPECfp  Info  Source
Name              (NUMx)Type ext/in  Ext+I/D    base95  base95  Date  Obtained
================= ========== ======= ========== ======= ======= ===== =========
DEC 250/4/266     A21064A    ??/266  2M+16/16     4.18    5.78  Apr95 www.dec
DEC 600/5/266     A21164     38/266  4M+96+8/8    6.43   10.64  Sep95 Digital
DEC 600/5/300     A21164     75/300  4M+96+8/8    7.33   11.59  Sep95 Digital
Intel Alder       PentiumPro 200     256+8/8      8.09    6.70  Nov95 www.intel
Intel XXpress     Pentium    66/133  1M+8/8       4.14    3.12  Nov95 www.intel

There are some lower end alphas which I couldn't find SPEC95 numbers for.
List prices for the DEC's run from $15k up to $40k for reasonably-configured
workstations:  Micron is offering a PentiumPro machine for $8k which is very
nice.  You can get a good pentium box for $3k.

For more on what these benchmarks mean, here's a copy of the SPEC FAQ
http://langmuir.eecs.berkeley.edu/www/peterm/SPECFAQ.html

and a copy of John DiMarco's table of SPEC benchmarks:
http://langmuir.eecs.berkeley.edu/www/peterm/SPEC.html

Regards,

PeterM



