>
>Hmmm...Not sure what the Alpha laptop was or was not designed to run nor
>whether or not that "design" was limited to simply some custom code in a
>boot chip. Biggest optical is marketing decisions and the decision to fund
>same for certain markets. Different business units within DEC have
>different overhead costs and business targets. 
>
>The Alpha laptop may be an example of this. Open VMS will be 1st as
>"supported" while NT may be last. All we can do is watch and wait....

The first Alpha laptop (a.k.a. Alpha LEAN) was designed to run WNT. So 
was its successor, the ill-fated Burns (a.k.a. Green Machine, and a 
rather ugly shade of green at that!). The AlphaBook 1 can support all 
three of DEC's OSes; OpenVMS support came first for a couple of reasons. 
First, early OVMS support signified DEC's "commitment" to the OS (at 
least that's what the marketeers said). Second, OVMS support renders the 
AlphaBook 1 unique: there are other RISC notebooks out there, but none 
that run OVMS.

Digital UNIX support will be forthcoming in the next few months. Which 
makes sense, since the AlphaBook appeals to government contractors and 
spooks who ply their clandestine trade in Unix. As for WNT, don't bet on 
it. Why would someone pay $15K for a Alpha WNT notebook when similar 
levels of performance are available on Pentium WNT notebooks for well 
under half the price?


