smithg@vitro.com (George Smith) wrote:
>A DEC MicroVAX system where my sister works is using a 95meg DLT       
>(I think) tape backup system which is taking nearly three hours 
>to back up about 300 meg of data.  My recommendation would be to
>upgrade to a 2gig minimum DLT system, which should increase the
>throughput significantly, and allow the backup to fit on one tape.
>
>I'm not real sure what might be available in a price range her boss
>could afford, and would like recommendations from anyone who may have
>more current info.  I've checked a couple of sites on the net, but all
>I can see is the 20gig unit, at $6000!  

A 95meg DLT would be a TK50.  Unfortunately, 100meg per hour is about 
right for this drive.

Not knowing anything about the system, my first recommendation would be 
a DAT (4mm) tape drive.  Unfortunately, a DAT drive has a SCSI 
interface, so you would need a SCSI controller.  Usually, the TK50 is 
found in a MicroVAX II system, which doesn't have a SCSI controller.  
You can find SCSI controllers for the MV II, but they tend to be a bit 
pricy (about $1500-$3000). You might be able to find a used one, 
however.

A DAT drive can back up about 10meg per minute.

Another possibility would be a TK70 drive. These drives hold about 
270meg and are considerably faster than a TK50. You should be able to 
get a used one (with controller) for a few hundred dollars.  However, 
you would need to confirm that the drive would work in the target 
system.

(Does anyone know if a TK70 will work in an MVII?)

Good luck!

