My advise is to put an inexpensive SCSI card in an expansion slot and 
hang a SCSI drive with its cab on the out side.  But if you really must 
try...

THERE ARE NO GUARANTEES THAT ANY OF THE FOLLOWING WILL WORK!!!!
THE FOLLOWING VOIDS YOUR WARRANTY, IS NOT SERVICABLE BY DIGITAL AND MAY 
CAUSE BODILY HARM ;^)

 DS1  - Jumper this if the drive is the replacement of the
        internal drive
    
 DS2  - jumper this if the drive is to be used in conjuction
        with an internal drive.
    
    Leave all other jumpers alone. Use FDISK to initialize the disk.

BIOS limitation of 1024 cylinders.

You will need a 2 drive cable (avail from local PC shop) and a 
Y-connector for the power.

    Configure the first drive as DS1 (the internal one). The outside
    one as DS2.  This is usually handled by jumpers on the drive itself.
    
    Remove the 'terminating resistor' from the bottom of the internal
    drive, and make sure one is installed in the outside drive.
    
    Run the Alt-Ctrl-Home setup routines to initialize the CMOS, the
    display should show "2 RD"
    
    Of course, this voids the warranty, is not field servicable,
    and all that stuff.





