In article <4cf07g$lru@nyx10.cs.du.edu>, pciszek@nyx10.cs.du.edu (Paul Ciszek) says:
>
>I have a DEC PDP-11/24 based data acquisition system and would like to be able
>to export files.  Does there exist a version of KERMIT written for the PDP 11?
>I would also have to be able to obtain it on an oversize floppy in the
>appropriate format.  (Or, alternatively, an RL02 platter, but I don't suppose
>anyone ships software on those.  ;-)
>
>Paul Ciszek                     "When the press is free and every man
>                                 able to read, all is safe."
>pciszek@nyx.net                                  --Thomas Jefferson

A PDP-11 version of kermit is available at kermit.columbia.edu
in the directory /kermit/bin.

It is possible to download kermit to the PDP-11 from another machine.
You transfer the .hex file, then type in a program that converts
this into an executable. This is documented in the help files that
come with k11????, so look there for the programs. You will need to
have some programming capability on the PDP-11 (I think BASIC and
MACRO are given).

If you set the serial port speed between the two machines slow enough,
you can copy the hex file to through the serial line, with
the PDP-11 in a PIP (or copy) from the serial line into
a text file. Else you may need to write a short program to feed it
one line at a time through the serial line, with a pause after each
line.

I've done it to RSTS/E a couple of times, but it's not much fun.
Once you get it running, make a good backup so you don't have to
go through it again.



