This is why you should have a chat with your lawyer and
your insurance company.   You may be "right", but it often
won't keep you from being sued.   The expense of defending
even one lawsuit would far outweigh the costs of using LMF.

Just as engineers can be sued if something they designs injures
someone, a programmer could be sued if they use practices that
"injure" a customer, if better practices were available.
Since LMF is available (and commonly used in the industry),
you might have a hard time winning this argument in front of
a jury.   I am not personally aware of any cases where an
individual programmer was singled out like this, but it
certainly is possible, depending on your state's laws.

Needless to say, I am not a lawyer, so you should consult
your own lawyer.

