For others who may have been wondering what it takes to package an
Alpha system from scratch, calling the local Digital Semiconductor rep
for Colorado, I got the following prices for 21164-based motherboards.

motherboard	  2000
2M cache card	+  900
266Mhz CPU	+ 1100
300Mhz CPU	+ 1700
--

System similar to an ALCOR/266 would be
$4000 + case + graphics + monitor + keyboard + mouse + memory + disk.

By the time you assemble all of that, you could call it ~$7000, or
about $7500 for a 300Mhz part. If you want better-than-sucky graphics,
it's about $900 or so.

This obviously doesn't include OSF licenses etc, but I'm assuming
you'd be using Linux or something.

By comparison, we recently put in a P.O. for a 180-Mhz P6 system from
Micro. The total price (with disk, 17" monitor, but no ethernet, no
SCSI) was around $4500. Allegedly, the P6-180 should be somewhat
comparable to an EV5-266 for SPECint, with EV5 better for SPECfp. I'll
be curious to see what the performance difference is whenever said P6
shows up.



